WEBVTT

1
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.160
Theatre – it can show us who we are,
who we were and who we could be.

2
00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.370
It means imagining bigger,

3
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.290
understanding better,

4
00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.620
feeling together –

5
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.370
on our stages and on tour,

6
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.250
in schools

7
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.000
and with communities,

8
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.500
on cinema screens worldwide

9
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.620
and now at home.

10
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.620
We are the National Theatre.

11
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.660
We make theatre for everyone.

12
00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:30.000
Art helps us to see the world in new ways.

13
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.120
The stories behind great works
help to bring them to life,

14
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.250
and hearing them shouldn't be a privilege.

15
00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.870
That's why we built Bloomberg Connects.

16
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.540
An app that lets you discover a growing
number of museums, gardens and parks,

17
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.910
behind-the-scenes interviews
and public art from around the world,

18
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.500
all in a single download
right in the palm of your hand.

19
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.870
Bloomberg Connects offers
a world of inspiration.

20
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.500
Join the National Theatre community
and hundreds of cultural institutions

21
00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.870
to get a deeper look into the world of art
through Bloomberg Connects.

22
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.370
(People chatting)

23
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.000
(Sombre instrumental music)

24
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.500
(Music intensifies)

25
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:46.830
(Music stops abruptly)

26
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.580
(Luka) I think I might be an incel.

27
00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.450
(Audience laughs)

28
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.950
- An incel?
- You know what an incel is?

29
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.700
It’s an American guy
who can’t get a girlfriend,

30
00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.080
then shoots up a school.

31
00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.580
(Audience laughs)

32
00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.200
Well, you don’t have to be American.

33
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:02.950
(Audience laughs)

34
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.330
Do you really think you have
that kind of capacity for violence?

35
00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.950
I can’t talk to women
without a drink in me.

36
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.450
- Have you tried?
- Yes.

37
00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.080
And what happens?

38
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.830
They’re not interested.

39
00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.700
- I find that hard to believe.
- Why?

40
00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.700
- You’re a good-looking guy.
- No, I’m not.

41
00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.830
You are.
You're a very handsome young man!

42
00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.700
I’m ginger.
Women don’t like gingers.

43
00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:26.830
(Audience laughs)

44
00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.700
You’re not ginger. You’re blond.
With a hint of golden honey.

45
00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:32.200
(Audience laughs)

46
00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.330
- Are you gay?
- Whoa, hey, come on now...

47
00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.830
- No, I don’t have a problem with it.
- No, I get it.

48
00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:41.580
I just don’t want you thinking
a blowjob’s on the cards.

49
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.950
Well, let me put your mind at rest, Luka.
I am not gay.

50
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.950
- OK.
- And like you,

51
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.330
- I don’t have a problem with it.
- I don’t have a problem with it.

52
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:50.950
(Mumbles)

53
00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:57.950
My first sponsor was gay.

54
00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.580
- Really?
- Yeah.

55
00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.700
He used to take me
to the gay meetings.

56
00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.700
- What are the gay meetings?
- AA meetings for gay people.

57
00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:07.450
(Audience laughs)

58
00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:08.950
- Gay AA?
- Yeah.

59
00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.950
(Audience laughs)

60
00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.580
- They have gay AA?
- Yeah, yeah.

61
00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:15.700
Why?

62
00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.330
I suppose
to make gay people feel safe.

63
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:19.580
That makes sense.

64
00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:23.450
The gay meetings were great.
The camaraderie, you know?

65
00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:27.700
They're not afraid to share anything,
the gays. Really let loose.

66
00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.450
I can’t imagine
having a gay sponsor.

67
00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.080
He was fearless.
And he made me fearless too.

68
00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.330
We could tell each other anything.

69
00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.950
- Did he try it on with you?
- No.

70
00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:39.950
What, never?

71
00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.950
Not all gay men fancy
all straight men, you know.

72
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.830
- Yes, they do.
- No, they don’t.

73
00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:47.950
- But all straight men fancy all women.
- Do they?

74
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.080
Most women I encounter, I think,
"Do I want to sleep with her?" Don't you?

75
00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.330
- I’m married.
- But you can still think it.

76
00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.580
The way I see it, every woman I meet
I think, "What’s she like in bed?

77
00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.080
What does she look like naked?"

78
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.830
So I assume every gay man
who meets me is thinking the same thing.

79
00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:06.200
- Assessing me for sex.
- Not everyone thinks like that.

80
00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.330
All men do.
Gay or straight. All men.

81
00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:09.700
I don’t think so.

82
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.700
He did try it on once.

83
00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:17.950
(Audience bursts out laughing)

84
00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.580
- What did he do?
- He touched my knee.

85
00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:22.330
- Sexual harassment.
- Nah.

86
00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.950
- I was flattered by it.
- Why were you flattered?

87
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.580
If a woman did it,
I’d be flattered.

88
00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:31.080
- Is that all he did?
- He also tried to kiss me.

89
00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:32.700
- (Audience laughs)
- He kissed you?

90
00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:33.950
- Tried to.
- Fucking hell...

91
00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.950
He was looking at my lips
like he wanted to kiss me.

92
00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.950
It's hard to believe, but I was very pretty
when I was your age.

93
00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:42.330
So what happened?

94
00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.450
He leaned in and I leaned away.

95
00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.330
He apologised
and we never mentioned it again.

96
00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.450
- You kept him on as your sponsor?
- He was a great sponsor.

97
00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:55.330
- Did you tell him sex stuff?
- Yeah, yeah.

98
00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:59.200
- Like what?
- Just about women I fancied.

99
00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:01.450
- Right.
- The idea is, with stuff like that,

100
00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.200
if you don’t tell someone,
your sponsor or whoever,

101
00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:06.080
- you’ll start drinking again.
- Right.

102
00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:08.580
It’s basic
fourth and fifth step stuff.

103
00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.450
- (Mumbles)
- The fourth step

104
00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.450
you make what’s called
"a moral inventory".

105
00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:16.330
A list of everything bad
you’ve done in your life,

106
00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.700
big or small. Resentments. Fears.
Harms done to others.

107
00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:24.080
If you’ve stolen money
from your mother, say,

108
00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.200
or tried to feel up some girl
when she’s passed out at a party.

109
00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:30.830
Whatever. You get it down on paper.

110
00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:34.580
And step five, you share it with someone,
your sponsor.

111
00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:38.580
The point is
to divest yourself of shame.

112
00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:41.700
Anything that’ll make you
pick up a drink in the future.

113
00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.830
It’s about getting clean.

114
00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.830
Like a confession
if you’re Catholic?

115
00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.830
It’s a similar, uh... Are you a Catholic?

116
00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:50.950
- No.
- OK.

117
00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.200
- I’m an atheist.
- Were you born Catholic?

118
00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:55.580
- My parents weren’t religious.
- OK.

119
00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.700
I think my grandpa
was a Free Presbyterian.

120
00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.700
I’m a recovering Catholic.

121
00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:00.830
(Audience laughs)

122
00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.450
- Right.
- I don’t think it’s all bad.

123
00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.330
It helps some people.
My wife goes to church sometimes.

124
00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:09.700
- It seems to help her.
- So are you an atheist?

125
00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:12.200
I would say I’m spiritual,
not religious.

126
00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:13.580
What does that mean?

127
00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:16.700
Oh. Um...

128
00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:17.830
(Audience laughs)

129
00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:21.700
- I dabble in Buddhism. The Tao.
- So then you’re not an atheist?

130
00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:24.950
Well, there’s no god in Buddhism.
You have to find your own path.

131
00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:28.700
- But they talk about God in AA.
- A god of your own understanding.

132
00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.330
- That’s step three.
- The third step, exactly.

133
00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:33.450
- It’s still God.
- Yeah, but it's like...

134
00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.700
that chair could be your god.

135
00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:37.580
I don’t get that.

136
00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:41.080
- A paper cup. Whatever.
- How could a paper cup be God?

137
00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.950
The point is you don’t have to believe
in the Christian God.

138
00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:45.450
It’s God as you understand him.

139
00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:47.830
I don’t get it.
I just don’t believe in God.

140
00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:50.830
Never have, never will.
Even if he is a paper cup.

141
00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:52.200
(Audience laughs)

142
00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:55.080
What is the point
in believing in a paper cup?

143
00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.200
Yeah, well, just...
Keep an open mind, you know.

144
00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.330
So, if I don’t believe in God,
am I going to get drunk?

145
00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:05.830
Well, some people believe you need
a spiritual awakening

146
00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:08.200
to have any hope of staying sober.

147
00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:11.700
If you read the Big Book, there was
a famous psychologist called Carl Jung.

148
00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:12.950
He believed that.

149
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:14.830
Do you think I need
a spiritual awakening?

150
00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:17.080
I think it helps,
but I’ve never had one.

151
00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:18.700
- Never?
- Mm.

152
00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.450
I suppose becoming a father
was a kind of awakening for me.

153
00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:24.330
Ah. How many kids do you have?

154
00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:27.700
Just the one. A son.
He’s at uni now in Bristol.

155
00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:29.700
- What’s he like?
- Oh, he’s terrific.

156
00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:33.580
We’re very close.
We can talk about anything.

157
00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:36.080
- That’s great.
- Yeah, he’s like my best friend.

158
00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.580
- What about your wife?
- What about my wife?

159
00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:41.950
- Is it nice to have a wife?
- Eh...

160
00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.700
(Audience bursts out laughing)

161
00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:47.080
It has its ups and downs,
like anything.

162
00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:49.330
- I'd like to have a wife someday.
- You will.

163
00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:50.450
And a coupla kids.

164
00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:51.950
- I’m sure you will.
- You think?

165
00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:54.330
If that’s what you want.
My sponsor used to say,

166
00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:57.580
"The programme offers you a life
beyond your wildest dreams."

167
00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:02.200
Now, I thought he meant
riches and material success.

168
00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:05.450
I’ve had some of that,
but really what AA has given me

169
00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.200
is the ability to be
an attentive father,

170
00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:12.950
a loving husband.
That was beyond my wildest dreams.

171
00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:16.330
I was hoping I could get
a woman sponsor.

172
00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:18.080
(Audience laughs)

173
00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:19.580
OK.

174
00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:21.700
I think I need a mother figure
in my life.

175
00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.950
I think that’s probably a mistake.

176
00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:26.080
- Why?
- Generally speaking,

177
00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:29.080
members of the opposite sex
shouldn’t sponsor each other.

178
00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:32.330
- Why not?
- Attraction becomes an issue.

179
00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:33.950
I see.

180
00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:39.950
I get the feeling your father
wasn’t a very positive role model.

181
00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:41.080
- My dad?
- Yeah.

182
00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:42.950
- My dad was a cunt.
- OK.

183
00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:45.700
- He used to beat my mum up.
- God, right...

184
00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:48.830
- In front of me when I was a kid.
- That's terrible. I’m sorry.

185
00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:50.950
- He died when I was 11.
- Was he a drinker?

186
00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.330
- Oh, aye. He was an alcoholic.
- It’s a family illness. It’s genetic.

187
00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:58.580
That’s why I go to the meetings...
I don’t want to end up like him.

188
00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:02.330
Good for you. And, listen,
I feel like you asking if I was gay...

189
00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:04.830
- I didn’t mean to offend you.
- No, you didn’t.

190
00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:08.330
But it's like you thought there was
some kind of transaction here.

191
00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:11.330
A blowjob.
Like I couldn’t like you for you.

192
00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:12.830
I get the impression, Luka,

193
00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:15.200
that every man
who’s ever been in your life

194
00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:16.580
has betrayed you

195
00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:19.700
so you have difficulty
trusting men.

196
00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:20.950
Older men.

197
00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:23.950
- Yeah.
- Mm?

198
00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:25.830
Yeah. Yeah, I do.

199
00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:29.330
So there you go. You need
a positive male role model in your life.

200
00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.330
One who’s not gonna
mess you around.

201
00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.700
So how many meetings
should I go to?

202
00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:37.700
As many as you can,
as often as you can.

203
00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:39.580
I was thinking of doing
six meetings a week.

204
00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:41.700
- Right.
- (Audience laughs)

205
00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:43.950
A meeting every day.
Gym in the morning,

206
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.450
look for work in the afternoon,
and a meeting at night.

207
00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:48.950
- Good plan.
- Go to the cinema on a Sunday.

208
00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:50.450
- Yeah, have a day off.
- Yeah.

209
00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:51.580
(Audience laughs)

210
00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:53.700
I love the movies.

211
00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:55.830
Ah, me too.
What kind of movies do you like?

212
00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:58.700
- Anything violent.
- Oh, right.

213
00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:00.080
(Audience laughs)

214
00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.700
- Like, horror. Extreme horror.
- I don’t like horror.

215
00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:05.450
- Korean horror. Japanese horror.
- I don’t like subtitles.

216
00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.950
Or anything gangster
like Goodfellas, The Godfather.

217
00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.200
Yeah, that’s more my kind of...
up my street.

218
00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:13.200
- Chinatown. Deer Hunter.
- Now we’re talking.

219
00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:15.950
Or like, Taxi Driver.
I love Robert De Niro.

220
00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:17.080
Me too. Raging Bull.

221
00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:19.330
- "You fuck my wife?"
- (Chuckles) Yeah.

222
00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:21.080
- "You fuck my wife?"
- That's good.

223
00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.200
"How can you ask me that?
Your own brother."

224
00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:25.950
- That's... Yeah.
- "You’re very smart, Joey.

225
00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:28.200
You give me all these answers
but you ain’t give me the right answers.

226
00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:30.580
I’mma ask you again.
You fuck my wife?"

227
00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:32.700
Yeah...

228
00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:38.330
Where were we? Um...
Yes. Uh, the important thing

229
00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:41.330
is to get involved with the programme
of recovery, the steps.

230
00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.700
- "The 12 Steps".
- That’s how you stay sober.

231
00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.330
Work the programme.
It works if you work it.

232
00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:49.700
But you can’t do it alone.
That’s why you need a sponsor.

233
00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:52.580
But how do I know
who to ask to be my sponsor?

234
00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:54.950
You want someone
with a bit of experience.

235
00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:58.580
I would advise ask someone
with at least ten years' sobriety

236
00:11:58.700 --> 00:11:59.830
under their belt.

237
00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:03.450
- Your sponsor, the gay one?
- Yeah?

